  "findings": [
    {
      "criterion": "Identity, markings, brightness",
      "status": "usable provisional source",
      "evidenceType": "manual local source pixels and actual-size playback samples",
      "finding": "All four drawings read as the intended adult male Western Bluebird: blue head/throat, blue wings/tail, rusty breast/flank/back region and pale lower belly. Large color regions remain readable at approximately60worldpx supported-settle width. Detailed feather shading is visible in enlargement but no conspicuous random pale interior sparkle or gross palette flash was apparent in the reviewed normal-size poses. Expert biological fidelity and rights remain unapproved."
    },
    {
      "criterion": "Real pose changes",
      "status": "pass as source study",
      "evidenceType": "manual source-pixel visual",
      "finding": "Crouch visibly compresses the legs/body and lifts the tail; airborne drawing curls both feet; descending drawing extends legs/toes; supported settle restores planted-looking feet. These are four real body drawings. Translation and root arc are separate playback transforms, not additional generated animation frames."
    },
    {
      "criterion": "Anatomy and alpha",
      "status": "minor retained caveats",
      "evidenceType": "manual original and enlarged feet",
      "finding": "No obvious extra major limb, detached foot or broken large body connection was apparent. Tucked toes overlap densely and feet/claws are angular at small output. A fine colored/translucent native edge fringe remains, more evident against black/enlarged than the green normal-size preview; no conspicuous thick normal-size halo was apparent. Alpha1 fringe omission is explicitly disclosed for derivatives; original is unchanged. I did not independently reconstruct the full nearest-component extraction, and do not claim every native sample survives derivatives."
    },
    {
      "criterion": "Normal-size hop usefulness",
      "status": "practical provisional study",
      "evidenceType": "seven corrected-viewer timestamped samples",
      "finding": "Samples show supported start, crouch, tucked airborne pose, descending legs and final support at a new horizontal position. The short action reads as a hop rather than static body bobbing. It is a discrete four-drawing study: takeoff and especially descending-to-settle switch abruptly, and it does not establish a smooth complete production cycle."
    },
    {
      "criterion": "Support and visible stance",
      "status": "physical foot lock unverified",
      "evidenceType": "manual normal/enlarged pixels plus authored-contact audit",
      "finding": "Root stationarity on support is verified, but toe shapes and stance spacing change during supported-settle to crouch. Declared lower-contour proxies move from plus/minus2.142857world relative to root in settle to plus/minus2.857143world in crouch:0.714286world per corresponding proxy. These are not tracked toe identities. At normal presentation the small stance redraw is subordinate to the crouch/tail movement; no assertion that both anatomical feet are locked is justified."
    },
    {
      "criterion": "Cross-original continuity",
      "status": "historical-neutral splice not approved",
      "evidenceType": "manual actual-size old/new side comparison",
      "finding": "The historical neutral has a more upright silhouette, longer downward tail presentation and a different head/torso balance than the newer horizontal settle. Compression alone does not establish seamless individual continuity; the observed mismatch is broader than the approximate52-versus42world bbox height difference. Keeping old neutral comparison-only and using this batch's own settle at both ends avoids that specific splice. Normal-yard entry/exit from the resident neutral remains unresolved."
    },
    {
      "criterion": "One-shot path and hold",
      "status": "pass for corrected source viewer",
      "evidenceType": "independent per-tick values plus corrected live canvas states",
      "finding": "All supported departure roots are[0,0]; all supported landing roots[20,0]. Only airborne ticks19–26 change root, with authored20world horizontal travel and8world root arc. The airborne drawing already lifts feet/body, so root arc is not total anatomical height. Corrected viewer samples at2200 and2500ms retain tick60/root[20,0],playing=false and identical final rasters; no silent loop reset was seen."
    }
  ],
